# Ask clear questions on pages and in dialogs

Ask only for what the task needs, one question at a time, and keep the person’s answers when they go back. Explain why you need an answer and never ask again for something they already gave.

These examples are local previews. They do not install Home Assistant screens or change a device.

## Compose the task without replacing its owner

This page owns clear questions, retained answers and the short-dialog adaptation. Other pages own the rest:

- [Complete multiple household tasks](complete-multiple-tasks.html) owns work with independent parts and saved progress.
- The [smart answer template](../publishing/frontend-templates/smart-answer.html) may route answers to guidance. It is not an automation or diagnostic engine.
- The [transaction template](../publishing/frontend-templates/transaction.html) explains a destination before opening it.

Home Assistant owns automation triggers, conditions, actions and supported setup flows. A question here does not replace those editors. A connected version needs its own route, permission, validation, saving and result handling. The previews prove none of these.

## Ask one understandable thing

Use the question as the label or group legend. Add a short hint that says what the answer changes. Offer “No room yet” or “I do not know” where that is a valid answer, so nobody has to invent one. Keep answers when the person goes back.

<%= example('nhs-question-room') %>

## Use a dialog for a short, contextual edit

Use a dialog for a small change, such as a display name, that belongs next to its control. Give it a visible heading and a Cancel button. Use a full page for a long form, several dependent steps, complex help or anything people may need to bookmark.

<%= example('nhs-question-modal') %>

- Move focus into the dialog when it opens.
- Keep focus inside while it is modal.
- Let Escape cancel.
- Return focus to the control that opened it when it closes.
- Let the dialog scroll at 320 CSS pixels and high zoom, with Close or Cancel still reachable.
- Do not rely on clicking the backdrop to leave.

See [Dialogs](../components/dialogs.html) for the full behaviour.

## Show errors without losing work

After a failed submit, show an error summary and a message beside each field. Keep what the person typed. Say whether the value is badly formed, the data is unavailable or they lack permission. Do not show an error before they have had a chance to answer.

<%= example('nhs-question-error') %>

## Keep it accessible

Link every label, hint and error to its input. Use real radios, checkboxes and inputs, not text that imitates them. Show a step count only when conditional branches make the total reliable. For a continuous value, keep native keyboard control and offer a typed value as an alternative to dragging. See [Sliders and precise values](../components/sliders.html).

## Respect Home Assistant behaviour

Keep the native entity controls. A display-name edit must not rename an entity unless the screen says it will. A brightness or temperature question must use the device’s real capabilities, units and range. Check permission and availability again before saving, then report the real result.

The previews only show local feedback. They do not save a profile, rename an entity or call a service. In a real dialog, close after saving only once the result is confirmed. Never apply an unsaved edit silently when the person closes the dialog.
